::Q01::Which statement best describes SQLite?{
~It is a database server that always requires a separate server process
=It is a lightweight, self-contained SQL database engine that can operate from a single file
~It is a Python-only data structure used to store variables
~It is a NoSQL database that cannot use SQL
}

::Q02::Which Python module provides built-in support for working with SQLite databases?{
~sql
~database
~sqlite
=sqlite3
}

::Q03::What is the role of a cursor object in Python's SQLite3 module?{
~It stores the database permanently in memory
~It creates the SQLite database file automatically
~It replaces the database connection
=It is used to execute SQL queries against the database
}

::Q04::Which method is used to create a connection to an SQLite database in Python?{
~sqlite3.cursor()
~sqlite3.execute()
=sqlite3.connect()
~sqlite3.open()
}

::Q05::[markdown]Which statement correctly describes `commit()` and `rollback()` in SQLite3?{
= `commit()` persists transaction changes, while `rollback()` reverts changes to the previous commit
~Both methods create a new database connection
~`commit()` retrieves query results, while `rollback()` retrieves only one result
~`commit()` closes the connection, while `rollback()` closes the cursor
}
